```
Late A event after B adopted would switch to A. Need guard. Add `supersededSessionRef` set.

In `adoptSession`:
```ts
const currentId = sessionIdRef.current;
if (currentId && currentId !== nextId) supersededSessions.current.add(currentId);
```
But if the event for A is current, adopt A no add. If B adopted from start response, A added. Late A ignored.

In `onSessionEnded`:
```ts
if (supersededSessions.current.has(parsed.data.sessionId)) return;
```
What if event for a brand-new B arrives before start response while current A? Not in superseded → handled, adopts B, adds A. Then start response B adopts B (same). Good.
What if event for an old A arrives after B adopted but A was not added because `adoptSession` returned early? When B is adopted, currentId A != B → add A. Good.
What if the app starts with no current and receives an event for A (before start), adopt A; no superseded. Then B start response adopts B, adds A. Good.
What if B start fails and A remains current? A event handled. Fine.
What if the same session A gets a second event after adopt A? Not superseded; handled again (idempotent stops). Fine.

Add ref:
`const supersededSessions = useRef<Set<string>>(new Set());`
In `adoptSession`, add after the identity check? If same id, return before adding. Do:
```ts
if (sessionIdRef.current === nextId) return;
if (sessionIdRef.current) supersededSessions.current.add(sessionIdRef.current);
sessionIdRef.current = nextId;
```
In onSessionEnded, guard before adopt.

